  'Molybdenum': {
    demand: 'Demand is on the order of ~260,000 metric tons annually.3',
    growth: 'Molybdenum demand is driven by: (1) infrastructure and energy transition, high-strength molybdenum-alloyed steels in wind turbines, pipelines, pressure vessels, and offshore structures; USGS expects "strong demand in global power generation and infrastructure projects as countries continue to prioritise clean energy"; (2) superalloys for jet engines and gas turbines (growing with aerospace recovery and defence); (3) catalysts in petroleum refining. CAGR estimated at 3–5%.2 3 5',
    diversity: 'Supply of molybdenum is dominated by a few mining countries. China produced ~110,000 t in 2024 = ~42% of world total ~260,000 t (USGS MCS 2025). Peru second at ~41,000 t (16%). Chile ~38,000 t (15%). USA ~33,000 t (13%). Mexico ~17,000 t (7%). China dominates molybdenum processing into ferromolybdenum and molybdic oxide. USGS 2025 Critical Minerals Methodology explicitly lists molybdenum as one of 17 minerals where China has implemented trade restrictions/bans, with 100% probability of restriction scenario. MOFCOM has explicitly identified molybdenum as restricted from export to the US. US imports ferromolybdenum primarily from Chile (77%) and Korea (19%), not from China, but this reflects current trade flows after restrictions.2 2 4',
    reserves: 'World molybdenum reserves are ~15,000,000 t (15 million t). At current production of ~260,000 t/year, reserves last ~58 years.China ~5,900,000 t = ~39% of 15 million t. USA ~3,500,000 t (23%). Peru ~1,900,000 t (13%). Chile ~1,400,000 t (9%). Russia ~1,100,000 t (7%). HHI ≈ 0.39² + 0.23² + 0.13² + 0.09² + ... ≈ 0.23. 5.52 7.5',
    enduse: 'Defense (+1.5): High-strength molybdenum steels in armour plate, naval vessels, artillery; molybdenum in high-temperature military engine alloys and missiles. Energy (+1.5): Molybdenum-alloyed pressure vessel and pipeline steels for nuclear reactors, petroleum refining, and renewables infrastructure. Construction (+1): HSLA (high-strength low-alloy) structural steel for infrastructure, bridges, buildings, offshore platforms. Electrification (+0.5): Molybdenum disulfide (MoS₂) as a dry lubricant and emerging 2D semiconductor. Healthcare (+0.5): Molybdenum-99 (Mo-99) is the parent isotope of Technetium-99m, the most widely used medical imaging radioisotope in the world (~40 million nuclear medicine procedures/year). Semiconductors (+0.5): MoS₂ in next-generation 2D transistors (early R&D). Agriculture (+0): nil.3 2 5',
    subst: 'Substitution for molybdenum is difficult in most of its major applications. In high-strength alloy and stainless steels, there is little practical substitute for molybdenum’s combination of strengthening and anti-corrosion effects. Potential substitutes include boron, chromium, niobium, and vanadium in alloy steels, all commercially deployed with moderate performance loss. Tungsten in tool steels is a commercial substitute. For Mo-99/Tc-99m medical imaging, there are some alternative radioisotopes but the infrastructure is globally centred on molybdenum. USGS notes molybdenum is recycled as a component of catalysts, ferrous scrap, and superalloy scrap. "The amount of molybdenum recycled as part of new and old steel and other scrap may be as much as 30% of the apparent supply." No separate refining of secondary molybdenum exists, it is captured within the steel and alloy recycling ecosystem.2',
    extraction: 'Molybdenum is mined as molybdenite (MoS₂) through flotation from porphyry copper-molybdenum deposits. Conversion to molybdic oxide requires roasting (thermal oxidation of MoS₂) at ~650°C. Ferromolybdenum production requires aluminothermic or thermite reduction. All these are well-documented industrial processes practised in 15+ countries.',
    projects: 'Yes. Several significant projects are underway or in advanced planning to expand supply. These include the ramp-up of the primary Climax mine in the USA, improved recovery in South America, and large potential primary mines like the Mt. Hope project in the USA.4 2 6',
    india: 'India has very limited domestic molybdenum resources and is almost entirely import-dependent. India is on the USGS list as a molybdenum producer at ~300 t (small; from Rajasthan vanadium-molybdenum deposits and Jharkhand copper-molybdenum occurrences). However, India imports the vast majority of its molybdenum requirements, primarily as ferromolybdenum for its steel industry. Ministry of Mines 2023 cites molybdenum as a critical mineral with import dependency >90% for processed forms.Molybdenum is on India\'s 30 Critical Minerals list. Budget 2024 exempts it from customs duty. GSI has been conducting exploration in Rajasthan (Alwar district) and Jharkhand for molybdenum. NCMM includes molybdenum in its exploration targets. The critical Mo-99/Tc-99m medical isotope programme at BARC (Mumbai) makes molybdenum a nuclear medicine security concern for India. 5',
    volatility: 'Prices are highly volatile, subject to significant spikes and corrections driven by supply disruptions, macroeconomic trends affecting electronics demand, and speculative trading. The tin market experienced one of the most dramatic single-event supply shocks in critical mineral history in 2023. In August 2023, the United Wa State Army (UWSA), the militia controlling Myanmar\'s Wa State region, home to the Mawchi mine complex, announced an abrupt halt to all mining and processing. The Mawchi mine produces approximately 70,000+ tonnes/year of tin-in-concentrate, representing ~11–13% of global production. The halt was initially expected to last months and ended up persisting nearly a year before partial resumption began in mid-2024. The LME tin price response was violent: prices, which had been around $26,000/t in mid-2023, spiked toward $38,000/t by November 2023, a ~46% jump in under six months. This was on top of earlier volatility: in early 2022, LME tin touched a record above $48,000/t (driven by post-COVID supply chains), then collapsed to $20,000/t by late 2022, a >50% decline in under a year. Total range over 2020–2024: from ~$15,000/t (2020 trough) to $48,000/t (2022 peak) to $38,000/t (2023 spike).44',
  },
