  'Rhenium': {
    demand: 'Demand is on the order of ~50-60 metric tons annually.4',
    growth: 'Rhenium demand is driven by two categories: (1) Superalloys for jet engine turbine blades (~80% of use), growing strongly with global aviation recovery, defence re-armament, and hypersonic weapon development. Second- and third-generation nickel superalloys require 3–6% rhenium for high-temperature creep resistance, and these are irreplaceable in military and commercial aero-engines. (2) Petroleum reforming catalysts (~15%), stable. Aerospace/defence superalloy demand growth is projected at 6–10% CAGR.2 3 5',
    diversity: 'Rhenium is produced as a byproduct of molybdenum roasting at porphyry copper-molybdenum mines. Chile produced ~29,000 kg in 2024 = ~47% of world total ~62,000 kg. Poland second at ~9,400 kg (15%), USA third at ~9,500 kg (15%), Uzbekistan ~5,000 kg (8%), China ~5,300 kg (9%).Rhenium refining/processing occurs at molybdenum and copper smelters in Chile, USA, Poland, Kazakhstan, Germany, and Canada. No single country dominates processing the way China does for other critical minerals. Chile supplies 44% of US imports; Canada, Germany, and Poland together supply ~40%. This is relatively well-distributed.2 2 4',
    reserves: 'USGS notes world reserves are "large", identified US resources alone are ~7 million kg (7,000 t). Total world reserves include Chile\'s 1,300,000 kg and Russia\'s 310,000 kg. At current production of ~62,000 kg/year, known reserves would last 25–100+ years depending on methodology. Additional rhenium is tied to undeveloped porphyry copper-molybdenum deposits globally.Chile holds ~1,300,000 kg = ~46% of identified reserves. USA ~400,000 kg (14%), Russia ~310,000 kg (11%), Kazakhstan ~190,000 kg (7%), Armenia ~95,000 kg (3%). HHI ≈ 0.46² + 0.14² + 0.11² + ... ≈ 0.25. 3.51 4.5',
    enduse: 'Defense (+1.5): Rhenium in single-crystal nickel superalloy turbine blades for military jet engines and hypersonic weapons, absolutely no viable substitute at the extreme temperatures required (>1000°C). USGS confirms "Rhenium improves the high-temperature (>1,000°C) strength properties of some nickel-base superalloys." Energy (+0.5): Petroleum reforming catalysts (stable use). Electrification (+1): Commercial aviation (superalloys in Airbus/Boeing engines) — growing with air travel. Healthcare (+0): None. Semiconductors (+0.5): Rhenium alloys in electron tubes, thermocouples, sensors. Agriculture (+0): None.4 2 6',
    subst: 'The market has moderate resilience. USGS lists substitutes but notes limitations: iridium-tin has achieved commercial success for catalysts; cobalt/tungsten for X-ray coatings; rhodium/iridium for thermocouples; tungsten for contacts. For the critical jet engine superalloy application, second and third generation single-crystal turbine blades, rhenium\'s unique effect on high-temperature creep strength has no commercially proven substitute at equivalent performance. First-generation alloys (no rhenium) are used in less demanding applications, but all high-performance military and modern commercial engines require rhenium-containing alloys. USGS notes a well-established recycling ecosystem: nickel superalloy scrap and turbine blades are recycled hydrometallurgically; spent petroleum reforming catalysts are recycled in a closed-loop system. Industry estimated ~25,000 kg of secondary rhenium produced worldwide in 2024, representing a significant share of global demand.3',
    extraction: 'The process is complex and specialized. Rhenium recovery requires catching perrhenate (ReO₄⁻) from the flue gases of molybdenum roasters at copper-molybdenum mines, a multi-stage scrubbing, ion exchange, and crystallisation process to produce ammonium perrhenate. The process requires co-location with molybdenum roasters at large porphyry copper mines.Complex, specialised; requires specific co-location; process is well-documented but capital-intensive; limited to ~15–20 facilities globally with the right feedstock.4',
    projects: 'Supply is structurally constrained by copper-molybdenum mine production. New copper mine development (where rhenium-bearing porphyry deposits are found) is the primary pipeline, but the copper sector\'s own pipeline is thin. Poland\'s KGHM has stable rhenium output from copper smelting. Chilean expansions add modest rhenium. No dedicated rhenium projects.5 3 8',
    india: 'India is 100% import-dependent for rhenium per Ministry of Mines 2023 report. India has no copper-molybdenum mines and no rhenium recovery infrastructure. India imports rhenium for superalloy production (defence and aerospace). CSEP 2025 confirms rhenium in the 10-mineral 100% import dependency list.Rhenium is on India\'s 30 Critical Minerals list (2023). Budget 2024 exempts it from customs duty. India\'s defence aerospace sector (jet engines for Tejas, Kaveri engine programme) requires rhenium-bearing superalloys. The National Critical Minerals Mission covers rhenium. However, no KABIL mandate specifically for rhenium, no foreign equity stakes in copper-molybdenum mines with rhenium recovery. 3',
    volatility: 'Prices have been rising sharply and are prone to sudden increases. Rhenium metal price rose from ~$1,030/kg (2020) to $1,370/kg (2024), a 33% increase over 4 years. APR (catalytic grade) price rose from $1,090/kg to $1,270/kg, a 28% increase. Within 2024, APR price jumped 38% year-on-year (from $920 to $1,270). Market is opaque (bilateral, no exchange), but relatively stable compared to other critical minerals because supply is geopolitically distributed (Chile, USA, Poland, Kazakhstan) without Chinese dominance or export restrictions.42.5',
  },
